plan Persistence
title   Persistence: durable actors and data on KeyValueStore
status  proposed
goal
    A Runtime with persistence keeps explicitly durable truth (persisted actors, persisted resource
    entries, confirmed entity records and the evidence of remote commits) in an Effect
    KeyValueStore under one scope and one version, and restores it on the next ready() without
    replaying executable work. Captures are one consistent cut with a closed durable context graph;
    each scope commits atomically; writes are ordered, coalesced and keep the last good record;
    corrupt or incompatible data is discarded with a diagnostic, never half-restored.

    This Plan owns configuration, the stored layout, capture, restore, the failure policy and attempt
    durability. Who may write a scope when several tabs share it is @plan.MultiTab.O1; what a
    server payload carries is @plan.ServerRendering.O3; dormant records of lazy entries are adopted
    by @plan.LazyFeatures.O3. The shared bounded codec is @plan.Inspection.O2 and the diagnostic
    vocabulary is @plan.Diagnostics.O2. Persistence does not exist in code today; stage S6 builds
    it greenfield, reusing the frozen package's bounded decoder limits, fail-closed version check,
    in-flight restore rules and newer-wins resource merge, and the rewrite's rule that restore
    requires persist: true. S6 depends on S4 for its core gate and on S5 for its entity gate
    (FlowState build stages).

    The rationale for the multi-tab parts is references/design/multi-tab.md sections 6 and 7; for
    dormant records, references/design/lazy-features.md section 8; for the hydration merge,
    references/design/server-rendering.md section 2.8. Where those designs and this Plan differ,
    this Plan wins.
outcomes
    O1  Configuration and storage
        runtimeSetup({ app, services, persistence?: { scope, version } }). persistence is optional;
        without it the Runtime performs no persistence I/O, captures nothing and restores nothing
        (@D1).

        - **scope**: a nonempty string under the authored identity rules (at most 256 UTF-8 bytes,
        no C0 controls, DEL, NUL or lone surrogates, exact spelling). The scope is the principal: a
        different user is a different scope and a new Runtime. Flow never derives or checks it.
        - **version**: a nonempty string under the same rules, chosen by the author. It is compared
        for exact equality; it has no ordering.
        - **storage**: the KeyValueStore service (effect/persistence, Effect 4.0.5) taken from
        services. With persistence given, the setup's Layer must provide KeyValueStore: a missing
        one is a type error (the requirement appears in the setup's R), and for untyped callers
        ready() fails with FlowError StorageFailure (details.reason "missing") before any other
        bootstrap step. It is never a silent no-op.

        The persistence object is closed: an unknown field is a type error and a construction
        FlowError (UnknownOption, @plan.Diagnostics.O2). runtimeSetup(...).construct() stays
        synchronous and inert: it acquires no storage, reads no key, computes no digest and starts
        nothing (@C4). Storage is acquired in ready(), before persisted data is validated and before
        app services are built (@plan.Runtime.O2).

        Flow ships one KeyValueStore Layer of its own, for IndexedDB (@D7), because Effect 4.0.5
        ships memory, file system, SQL and Web Storage layers only:

        - **indexedDbKeyValueStore({ database? })**: Layer<KeyValueStore>. database defaults to
        "flow-state"; the database has one object store "kv" at IndexedDB schema version 1 and no
        upgrade path. Each get, set, remove, has, size, modify and clear is one IndexedDB
        transaction, so one set is atomic per key. The database is opened lazily on the first
        operation; the Layer itself never fails, and an open failure (private browsing, blocked
        upgrade, quota) surfaces as KeyValueStoreError on every operation, which @O5 maps to
        StorageFailure. Transactions of a destroyed document abort, so no write outlives its page
        (required by @plan.MultiTab.O1). Values are stored as strings exactly as given.

        KeyValueStore.layerStorage(() => localStorage) remains the small synchronous choice (about 5
        MB per origin); the IndexedDB Layer is the documented choice for entity record stores. Any
        KeyValueStore is accepted, under the store requirement of @C5. Story runs never use the
        setup's storage (@D12): Testing installs a fresh in-memory KeyValueStore per Story run in
        place of the setup's, shared by every tab of that Story, which a fixture may seed
        (@plan.Testing.O3).

        Keys: every key Flow writes starts with the scope prefix flow-state/1/<digest>/, where
        digest is the lowercase hex SHA-256 of the length-prefixed pair (app id, scope) under the
        composite identity encoding of the Runtime's identity rules. The scope therefore never
        appears in plain text in storage or in lock names. Flow bundles its own SHA-256 (no Web
        Crypto dependency, because insecure origins lack crypto.subtle). Flow never calls
        KeyValueStore.clear and never reads, writes or removes a key outside its prefix (@C3).

        A Runtime writes its scope only while it is the scope's writer: always when no tab
        coordination applies (solo), and only while it is the leader when it does
        (@plan.MultiTab.O1). A follower reads only the store part (@plan.MultiTab.O2); a degraded
        Runtime and a Runtime whose restore read failed never write (@O5).
        Reference (references/dispatch-desk): app/persistence.ts (KeyValueStore.layerStorage, scope
        per user, version "1").
    O2  What is persisted
        Nothing is durable unless declared; every default is false (@D2).

        - **Actors**: actorRef(machine, id, { persist: true }) selects that shared actor. Local
        actors (createActor, useActor) are never persisted. A persisted ref's machine must have a
        Memory Schema, and Schemas for every internal event a pending outcome can carry, that
        encode and decode synchronously with no service requirements (Schema.Codec<A, I, never,
        never>): otherwise actorRef is a type error (@D8).
        - **Resources**: resource({ persist: true }) selects every entry of that descriptor holding
        confirmed data at the cut (lane Ready or Refreshing, or Failure with previous data). It
        requires the descriptor's result Schema, under the same purity rule; persist: true without
        result is a type error and a construction FlowError (@plan.Operations.O1). Entries with no
        data, placeholder data and optimistic layers are never persisted.
        - **Transactions** take no persist option (a type error): a commit is persisted as part of
        its actor's record when, and only when, that actor is persisted (@D11).
        - **Streams**: stream({ persist: true }) persists the latest value of that stream's lanes in
        persisted actors, encoded with the stream's result Schema, which persist: true requires
        (@D11). A stream lane of a persisted actor without persist: true persists only its terminal
        fact (@O4), never a value.
        - **Entity records**: only confirmed data. For each persisted answer (an entry of a
        persist: true resource registered in a domain), its references and context are persisted,
        and for each record only the fields that persisted answers project, encoded with the
        entity's field Schemas. Nothing persisted is a prediction (@plan.EntityDomains.O2).

        Selection is per declaration and never transitive: a persisted resource never makes an
        actor durable, and a persisted actor never makes a resource durable; resource entries an
        actor reads are persisted only through the resource's own flag. There is no filter, no
        per-instance exclusion and no automatic durability.

        A persisted actor's record holds exactly:

        - its ref identity (machine id and the id parts in their length-prefixed encoding) and the
        ref's declared options as captured: persist, retain and each context slot's provider ref
        identity, with whether the wiring came from the ref or from the app default;
        - its lifecycle (active or disposing) and state path;
        - its memory, encoded with the definition's Memory Schema;
        - each armed schedule: its name, whether it is a Duration or a multi-step Schedule, its
        absolute deadline in Runtime Clock epoch milliseconds, and whether a restartOn schedule is
        still idle (@plan.Machines.O5);
        - each activity binding: declaration slot, operation id, canonical key, and whether the
        binding had delivered its value outcome for the entry's current confirmed value;
        - each stream lane's terminal fact (Complete, Failure or Defect) and, with stream persist,
        its latest value;
        - each pending commit: transaction id, canonical key, attempt (encoded with the Attempt
        Schema of @plan.Operations.O3), ordinal, phase (queued or invoked) and the entity keys it
        predicts (@O6);
        - each settled commit outcome event that no turn has decided yet, encoded with the
        definition's internal event Schema, in mailbox order;
        - its counters: turn revision, commit ordinals and schedule generations, as non-negative
        safe integers.

        Never persisted: mailbox contents other than those settled commit outcomes (admitted but
        undecided public events, schedule firings and context wakeups are lost on reload, by
        design), the before-attach send buffer, snapshot context (it is recomputed from providers),
        issues (they describe the previous Runtime; @D10), raw Cause, observed provider revisions,
        fibers, scopes, callbacks, services, sessions, transports, buffered stream emissions,
        commit params beyond what the Attempt carries, input, and any executable work.

        Each persisted uncertain entity write is also stored as its own plain-data record (entity
        id, canonical key, attempt id), separate from its actor's record, so the uncertainty marker
        applies no later than the first moment the record is readable even when its actor is
        dormant (@O4).
        Reference (references/dispatch-desk): features/Session/session.ref.ts and
        features/Tickets/editor/editor.ref.ts (persist: true), features/Tickets/ticket.operations.ts
        (ticketById persist: true with result; technicianUpdates persist: false; saveTitle without
        persist).
    O3  Capture and writes
        **The cut.** After each publication that changes persisted content, the scope's writer
        prepares a capture: it observes the canonical store once and reads every selected actor's
        published state atomically, between completed publications, never inside one
        (@plan.Machines.O3). Each persisted actor's observed store revision is no newer than the
        captured store revision. A publication that changes only non-persisted content (an issue,
        a lane of a non-persisted stream, a local actor) schedules no capture.

        **Membership.** Registered persisted actors that are active or disposing, plus the exact
        transitive closure of their context providers; persisted resource entries; entity records
        as @O2 selects them; uncertainty markers. Disposed actors and actors whose retain expired
        are absent from the next capture, so disposing a persisted editor forgets its draft.
        Dormant records (@O4) are carried forward byte-identical: capture never rewrites, decodes
        or drops a record whose owner is not adopted.

        **Closure.** Every provider in a persisted actor's closure must itself be persisted and
        must have completed context propagation for the cut:

        - A required provider that is local, non-persisted or absent makes the whole consumer
        closure (that consumer and every persisted actor that reads it, transitively) fail
        NonDurableContextProvider. This is terminal for that closure for the Runtime's life: its
        records are omitted from every later capture, the rest of the scope keeps persisting, and
        each affected actor carries the diagnostic as an issue. Flow never promotes, substitutes,
        recreates, rebinds or silently omits a provider. One diagnostic per failing binding, each
        with details consumer ref, consumer machine, provider ref (or "local"), provider machine
        and path context.<slot>; several failing bindings are all reported; remediation names
        actorRef(providerMachine, id, { persist: true }). flow check reports the same case
        statically (@plan.CommandLine.O5).
        - Context debt (a provider wave not yet settled in a consumer) makes the capture fail
        ConcurrentCapture. It is retryable and internal: the writer retries at the next settled
        cut, records evidence for inspector sinks and traces, and never writes a stale or partial
        cut. It is not an issue.

        Capture performs no external work, emits no context event, starts no lookup and mutates no
        Runtime truth. It is distinct from inspector sinks (@plan.Inspection.O3).

        **Encoding.** Each value is encoded with its Schema (@O2) and then with the shared bounded
        structural codec (@plan.Inspection.O2) to canonical JSON text: depth 32, 10,000 nodes,
        4,096 array items, 262,144-byte strings, 2 MiB canonical size per record. A value that
        fails its Schema or exceeds a bound fails the capture with CodecFailure naming the path and
        the bound; nothing is written for that cut (no partial cut), the last good record stays,
        and no Runtime mutation happens. Encoding never stringifies, coerces or drops a value.

        **Layout (@D3).** A scope is a set of records named by a manifest:

        - **records**: one per persisted actor; per persisted resource descriptor, per entity and
        for uncertainty markers, as many chunks as needed, filled in canonical key byte order so
        that each stays within the codec bounds. A record keeps its key while its bytes are
        unchanged; a changed record is written under a fresh key flow-state/1/<digest>/r/<g>/<n>
        for generation g.
        - **manifest**: format 1, app id, scope digest, version, generation g (a safe integer
        strictly increasing per scope), capturedAt (Runtime Clock epoch milliseconds) and the
        record list (key, owner kind, owner id, chunk index, byte length, SHA-256 of the bytes),
        at most 4,096 records.
        - **two slots**: generation g is written to manifest slot flow-state/1/<digest>/m/<g mod
        2>, so the previous generation's manifest stays readable in the other slot.
        - **scope bound**: at most 4,096 records and 16 MiB of record bytes; a capture exceeding
        either fails with CodecFailure (details.bound) and keeps the last good record.

        **Write order.** A write sets every new record key, then the manifest slot, then, after the
        manifest set is acknowledged, removes the record keys named by neither generation g nor
        g − 1. A reader therefore sees one complete cut without a lock: the newest manifest whose
        named records are all present with matching length and digest. A torn set (a missing or
        mismatched record, or an undecodable manifest) is ignored and the other slot's manifest
        wins. Store requirement: one KeyValueStore set is atomic per key (@C5).

        **Ordering and coalescing.** Writes are serialised per scope: at most one write is in
        flight, and while it runs only the latest prepared cut waits, replacing any older waiting
        cut. A cut is prepared at the end of the scheduler task that published it, so a burst of
        turns in one task produces one write; there is no debounce timer. An older write never
        replaces a newer one: generations only increase and a waiting cut is always the newest. A
        remote commit's pre-invocation write is never coalesced away (@O6).

        **Flush.** On page hide (visibilitychange to hidden, and pagehide, delivered by
        @plan.MultiTab.O8's lifecycle stream) the writer writes the latest prepared cut,
        synchronously when the store is synchronous (Web Storage). That is best effort; the
        guarantee stays the last good record. On runtime.dispose() the writer stops observing after
        admission closes and in-flight commits settle (a commit that never settles within the
        disposal bound stays recorded as invoked, so it restores as unknown), captures the final cut,
        finalises that write, and only then releases the store; dispose waits for it
        (@plan.Runtime.O6). runtime.dispose({ forget: true }) removes the scope instead of writing
        it (@plan.MultiTab.O6): it removes both manifest slots first, then every record key either
        manifest named, so a reader never sees a manifest without its records.
        Reference (references/dispatch-desk): app/persistence.ts (the NonDurableContextProvider note:
        a durable editor needs a durable session).
    O4  Restore
        Restore runs inside the shared bootstrap of runtime.ready() (@plan.Runtime.O2), in this
        order:

        1. Acquire storage (the KeyValueStore service).
        2. Read both manifest slots, then every record the selected manifest names, bounded by the
        scope bound of @O3. A KeyValueStoreError on any read fails nothing: the Runtime runs
        without persistence for its life (it never writes, so it cannot overwrite data it could not
        read) and reports StorageFailure (@O5).
        3. Validate and prepare detached data, before app services are built and before anything
        is installed: decode with the codec bounds and the manifest and record Schemas; check
        format, app id, scope digest and version for exact equality; check each record's length
        and digest; validate every adopted owner's records against the receiving compiled app.
        4. Build app services.
        5. Merge with hydrated items when a server payload was applied before ready (persisted
        actors beat hydrated actors; hydrated data beats restored data; @plan.ServerRendering.O5).
        6. Install the prepared actors, one canonical store with the restored resource entries and
        entity records, and the uncertainty markers, providers before consumers.
        7. Establish silent context baselines from the restored providers: selected context is
        recomputed, never read from storage, and no onContext event is manufactured.
        8. Drain each restored actor's persisted pending outcomes as its first turns, then
        reconcile its activities and re-arm its schedules.
        9. Expose handles and resolve ready().

        A failure or interruption at any step exposes no actor, handle or external work, and
        releases what was acquired in reverse order (@plan.Runtime.O2). Restore of a persisted
        record never fails ready(): invalid data follows @O5.

        **Validation against the receiving app.** For each actor record: the machine id is an eager
        machine of the app (a declared lazy entry id makes it dormant; any other id is an orphan);
        the id parts are valid names; the state path exists; memory decodes with the Memory Schema;
        every schedule name, activity slot, transaction id and pending outcome event exists in the
        definition and decodes; every context slot exists and its provider is in the record set;
        when the wiring came from the app default, it equals the app's current default; counters
        are safe integers that leave the headroom of @plan.Runtime.O4. For each resource and entity
        record: a known operation or entity id decodes with its Schemas; an unknown id stays
        dormant. Any failure of an adopted owner's record, or an orphan actor record, discards the
        scope (@O5).

        **Actors.** A restored actor is installed as restored: initial never runs, input is never
        read, and no event, turn or finite work is replayed. It is active at ready() and owned by
        the Runtime without any ensureActor: it is captured again, its retain clock (if any) starts
        at ready, and it lives for the Runtime, until retain expires or until disposeActor. A later
        ensureActor(ref, options) returns the restored actor and ignores input (the restored actor
        counts as the first accepted input); the ref's declared options must equal the captured
        ones, or ensure rejects with the ref-conflict FlowError of @plan.Runtime.O3 (@Q1). A restored
        actor that was disposing finishes disposing.

        **Pending outcomes and commits (@plan.Operations.O3).** Per actor, in this order and before
        any turn admitted from outside:

        - the persisted settled outcome events, in their captured mailbox order;
        - each pending commit in ordinal order: invoked restores as the commit's unknown outcome
        carrying its attempt; queued restores as its interrupt outcome, which is honest because a
        remote commit of a persisted actor is never invoked before a write recording it as invoked
        is acknowledged (@O6). The absence of an invoked fact in an older record never proves
        non-invocation on its own; only that write-before-invoke rule does.

        A restored unknown commit has no optimistic layer (layers are never persisted); its entity
        keys are marked uncertain and get the forced repair of @plan.EntityDomains.O2. Reconciling
        is workflow: the machine handles outcomes.unknown as a normal internal event and re-issues
        the same attempt.id with retry(attempt); Flow never mints a new id for reconciliation.
        Authors should give unknown an actionable exit (an uncertainty state, as the editor's
        UNCERTAIN), never leave it in SAVING.

        **Schedules (@D9).** A Duration schedule keeps its absolute deadline; an overdue one fires
        once at activation, never a burst. A multi-step Schedule restarts from its first step. A
        restartOn schedule keeps idle-or-armed as captured and takes a silent baseline from the
        restored snapshot, so restore never fires it by itself.

        **Resources.** Restored entries are installed at store position 0 and count as stale at
        restore whatever their age, so live data wins: an active binding refetches on activation
        and its lane shows the restored data as Refreshing. In-flight lookups never resume. An
        entry no live binding holds stays passive (no lookup) until one binds, and its gcTime
        starts at ready. Pinned parameters and the oldest live binding for automatic refetch apply
        as for any entry (@plan.Operations.O2). A binding that had delivered its value outcome for
        the restored value delivers nothing again; one that had not delivers it once on activation;
        a later confirmed change delivers under Equal.equals (@plan.Operations.O6).

        **Entity records.** Restored at position 0 with every restored answer's frontier lowered,
        so live data wins; uncertainty markers apply at ready, or at the first adoption that makes
        their entity known, whichever comes first, and mark the record uncertain and run the
        forced repair (@plan.EntityDomains.O2).

        **Streams.** A lane that was running opens a fresh session after the actor's pending
        outcomes drain, keeping its persisted latest value as data until the first new value; old
        emissions are never replayed. A lane with a terminal fact (Complete, Failure or Defect)
        restores in that tag and does not resubscribe until its activity identity changes.

        **Counters** restore at their captured values and continue upward; they never reset. A
        record whose counters lack the reserved headroom fails validation before installation.

        **Dormant records.** Bootstrap reads the whole scope. Records whose owner is not adopted
        (an actor of a lazy machine, an unknown operation or entity id) are held as bytes keyed by
        owner id and decoded only when @plan.LazyFeatures.O3 adopts their owner. Unknown operation
        and entity ids stay dormant until a version change discards the scope. A dormant consumer
        gets a fresh baseline from current providers at adoption.

        **Followers.** A tab that is a follower installs only the store part of the last good
        record, read-only (@plan.MultiTab.O2); a new leader after handover runs this restore
        unchanged (@plan.MultiTab.O4).
        Reference (references/dispatch-desk): app/persistence.ts (what a reload restores),
        features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts (unknown: E.SaveUncertain, retry(attempt), the
        UNCERTAIN state).
    O5  Failure policy
        Every failure is a FlowError diagnostic with a closed code from @plan.Diagnostics.O2,
        classification Failure, scalar details and remediation; no raw parser, Schema or storage
        exception, generic "corrupt" result or undefined escapes, and raw Cause never enters
        storage. The codes, distinguishable from each other:

        - **StorageFailure**: a KeyValueStore operation failed (details.operation get, set or
        remove; details.reason missing, quota, unavailable or other).
        - **CodecFailure**: a value failed its Schema encode or a codec or scope bound at capture
        (details.path, details.bound).
        - **IdentityVersionMismatch**: the stored format, app id, scope digest or version differs,
        an actor record names an orphan machine, or a captured app-default wiring differs
        (details.expected, details.found, details.owner).
        - **MalformedData**: stored bytes fail the codec bounds, a Schema decode, a length or digest
        check, or a counter headroom check (details.owner, details.path, details.bound).
        - **ConcurrentCapture**: retryable, internal (@O3).
        - **NonDurableContextProvider**: terminal per closure (@O3).

        Policy (@D4):

        - **Restore read failure**: run without persistence for the Runtime's life; StorageFailure.
        ready() succeeds.
        - **Version mismatch, orphan or corrupt data at ready()**: discard the whole scope's records
        (all actor, resource, entity and marker records, dormant ones included), start fresh, and
        report IdentityVersionMismatch or MalformedData once. ready() succeeds. Compatible shape
        changes are expressed as Schema decode transformations; there is no migration API in
        version 1. Remediation: bump persistence.version when stored data changes shape. Storage
        keeps the old bytes until the first successful write of the fresh Runtime, whose generation
        is one more than the highest generation found, so slots never collide.
        - **Torn newest generation**: not a failure; the previous slot's manifest is restored.
        - **Dormant record failing at adoption**: discard that adoption closure's dormant records
        only, with the restore codes; adoption itself does not fail (@plan.LazyFeatures.O3).
        - **Write failure**: the committed turn stays committed and never fails; the last good
        record stays; StorageFailure is reported once per distinct reason until a write succeeds;
        the next publication retries with the newest cut.
        - **Encode failure**: CodecFailure; that cut is not written; no Runtime mutation.
        - **Pre-invocation write failure**: the commit is not invoked and settles through its
        interrupt outcome, with StorageFailure (@D6).

        **Where it is reported.** Every diagnostic goes to inspector sinks and traces as evidence
        (@plan.Inspection.O4). A write-side condition (StorageFailure on write, CodecFailure,
        NonDurableContextProvider) also appears as an issue on each affected persisted actor's
        snapshot and clears on the next successful write; such issue-only publications schedule no
        capture, so a failing store cannot loop. Restore-side discards are evidence only; ready()
        still succeeds. Cancellation and cleanup stay typed with honest failure evidence
        (@plan.Runtime.O6).
        Reference: none yet (stage S10 adds it).
    O6  Attempt durability
        For a persisted actor, Flow records a remote commit's attempt before the adapter is invoked:
        the commit's invocation waits for an acknowledged write of a cut that shows the commit
        invoked with its attempt (phase invoked, @O2). This write is a forced flush, never
        coalesced away, and it carries every other change of that cut. Only then is the adapter
        called with { signal, attempt }. A non-persisted actor's commits have no durability: they
        are invoked at once, and their workflow is lost on a crash like the rest of that actor.

        Consequences:

        - On restore, a commit recorded as invoked restores as unknown with the same attempt, and
        a commit recorded as queued restores as interrupt (@O4). Both are delivered as ordinary
        turns before outside input.
        - The unknown outcome carries the attempt; reconciliation re-issues the same attempt.id
        through retry(attempt) (@plan.Operations.O3). Flow never mints a new id for a
        reconciliation and never retries by itself.
        - If the pre-invocation write fails, the commit is not invoked: it settles through its
        interrupt outcome and StorageFailure is reported (@D6). The adapter is never called with an
        attempt Flow could not record.
        - Under several tabs, only the leader runs persisted actors, so only the leader invokes and
        records their commits; an attempt is reconciled by exactly one Runtime, the one that
        started it or the leader that restores it (@plan.MultiTab.O4).
        - attempt.id is random per attempt in production and seeded in Stories (per tab and tab
        incarnation under several tabs), so a restored or reopened Runtime never reuses an id.
        Reference (references/dispatch-desk): features/Tickets/ticket.operations.ts (saveTitle passes
        attempt.id as the idempotency key), features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts (retryUncertain).
decisions
    D1  Persistence uses KeyValueStore with scope and version
        Was FlowState D41, adopted from minimal-library decision 10. runtimeSetup takes persistence:
        { scope, version } and the KeyValueStore service from its Layer, imported from
        effect/persistence (not effect/unstable/persistence). Replaces SurfaceSimplification D21's
        storage and codec object and the archived PersistenceStorage, webStorage, indexedDbStorage,
        persistence(), PersistenceCodec, filter, PersistenceSlot, PersistenceEntry,
        PersistenceValue and FlowPersistenceError. Effect owns the storage concept (FlowState D6).
    D2  Durability is explicit declaration ownership
        Kept from archived Persistence D1. Separate actor, resource and stream opt-ins select data;
        every default is false; no selection is transitive; restore retains data and identity,
        never authority to replay work. The archived exclusion-only filter is deleted: per
        declaration flags are the single selection source.
    D3  Each scope commits atomically through a manifest written last
        Binding ruling 17. Records are written first under fresh keys; a manifest naming the
        record set is written last into one of two alternating slots; a torn set is ignored and the
        last good manifest wins. Replaces the archived "one logical record per Runtime and scope",
        which a multi-key store cannot write atomically, and serves multi-tab readers that read
        without the lock and lazy dormant records that must be carried forward untouched.
    D4  Version mismatch and corrupt data discard the scope
        Binding ruling 17, settling the archive's silence on discard versus failure. A mismatched
        version, an orphan identity or corrupt data discards that scope's records with a
        diagnostic and the Runtime starts fresh; ready() never fails because of stored data.
        Compatible shape changes go through Schema decode transformations. No migration API in
        version 1.
    D5  Restore and writes belong to one Runtime lifecycle
        Kept from archived Persistence D2, with the bootstrap order of the Sol plan review F1:
        storage is acquired first, persisted data is validated detached, then app services are
        built, then data is installed. No partial bootstrap is published. Writes are serialised and
        the last good record survives any failure, independently of turn success.
    D6  A persisted actor's remote commit is recorded before it is invoked
        Binding ruling 6. The pre-invocation write makes "queued at restore" proof of
        non-invocation, which the archive could not give ("a saved not-yet-invoked fact covers
        only its cut"). Decided in this Plan: when that write fails the commit interrupts rather
        than invoking unrecorded, because an unrecorded invocation would restore as interrupt and
        lie about a request that may have reached the server.
    D7  Flow ships an IndexedDB KeyValueStore Layer
        Binding ruling 18. Effect 4.0.5 has no IndexedDB layer, and Web Storage alone (about 5 MB,
        synchronous) is too small for entity record stores. The Layer never fails to build; open
        failures surface per operation, so private browsing degrades to "run without persistence"
        instead of failing ready(). Its route is @plan.Package.O1.
    D8  Persisted Schemas are synchronous and requirement-free
        Restored from archived Persistence O1 ("the application codec validates synchronously and
        purely"). Effect 4 Schemas may carry effectful or service-requiring transformations; every
        Schema that persistence encodes or decodes must have none, so restore stays detached before
        services. A violation is a type error at the selecting declaration.
    D9  Schedules keep their absolute deadlines across restore
        Binding ruling 10. A Duration keeps its deadline and fires once if overdue; a multi-step
        Schedule restarts from its first step; restartOn takes a silent baseline. Replaces the
        archived timer due and consumed activation facts, deleted with timers (FlowState D23).
    D10  Issues, snapshot context and observed revisions are not persisted
        Decided in this Plan, changing archived Persistence O2's actor fact list. Issues describe
        the previous Runtime and would show stale failures after a reload; context is recomputed
        from restored providers (archived rule); observed revisions serve only the capture
        consistency check within one Runtime. The server payload makes the same choice
        (@plan.ServerRendering.O3).
    D11  Transactions follow their actor; streams opt in for their latest value
        Binding ruling 15, resolving FlowState O5's inconsistent persist? on transaction() and the
        reference's persist: false on a stream. transaction() has no persist; a commit is durable
        exactly when its actor is. stream({ persist: true }) persists the latest value and requires
        result; resource({ persist: true }) requires result.
    D12  Stories never touch real storage
        Restored from archived Persistence O1 ("fixtures and story.machine inherit no browser or
        session storage"), and required by Story determinism. Testing installs a fresh in-memory
        KeyValueStore per Story run in place of the setup's (@plan.Testing.O3).
    D13  The scope's writer is decided by tab coordination
        Binding ruling 32, lifting archived Persistence C1's exclusion of a same-scope writer
        protocol. A scope has one writer: the solo Runtime, or the leader tab
        (@plan.MultiTab.O1). This Plan's capture, writes and restore are single-Runtime rules that
        the writer applies unchanged. FlowState O8's "a second tab runs without persistence" is
        kept only as the degraded role (@plan.MultiTab.O7).
constraints
    C1  No migration, fingerprint, boot decoder, mutable hydration or filter API
        Kept from archived Persistence C1, minus the cross-tab exclusion (@D13). There is no public
        migration hook, fingerprint, boot decoder or payload, mutable hydrate on the Runtime, filter
        or automatic durability. The one sanctioned server-to-client path is dehydrate and
        <Hydrate> (@plan.ServerRendering.O1); traces, checkpoints and artifacts are never restore
        inputs.
    C2  Restore is exact and non-executable
        Kept from archived Persistence C2. No actor aliases, actor-private cache copies, serialised
        selected context, implicit retry, terminal-stream restart or reconstruction of parameters
        from keys. Every structural byte uses the shared codec bounds (@plan.Inspection.O2).
    C3  Flow touches only its own keys
        Flow never calls KeyValueStore.clear and never reads, writes or removes a key outside the
        scope prefix flow-state/1/<digest>/. Other application data in the same store is safe.
    C4  Construction is inert
        construct() performs no storage I/O, digest computation or work; everything starts in
        ready() (@plan.Runtime.O2).
    C5  Store requirements
        A KeyValueStore used for persistence must make one set atomic per key and must not let a
        write outlive its page. Web Storage, the IndexedDB Layer and the memory store meet both. A
        remote or process-external store whose writes can complete after the page dies is not safe
        for several tabs and is documented as such; solo assumes an unshared store.
verification
    V1  Configuration, types and defaults
        Establish @O1 and @O2 with type fixtures (persistence without KeyValueStore in R, persist
        on transaction, persist: true without result, an impure or service-requiring Memory or
        result Schema, an unknown persistence field: each rejected) and runtime tests: construct()
        performs zero storage operations (a counting store), every declaration defaults to
        non-durable, resource persist never selects an actor and actor persist never selects a
        resource, untyped missing storage fails ready() with StorageFailure, and a Story never
        touches the setup's store (a store that throws on any use). Planned.
    V2  Bootstrap and restore validation
        Establish @O4's order and validation: restore compatible records; restore records with a
        changed version, app id, orphan machine, removed state, renamed schedule, changed
        app-default wiring and malformed memory, each discarding the scope with its code while
        ready() succeeds; inject failure (read error, interruption, a service Layer failure) at
        each bootstrap step and assert no handle, actor or external work and reverse-order
        release; a restored actor is captured again without ensureActor; ensure on a restored ref
        ignores input; a conflicting ref rejects. Planned.
    V3  Capture closure and race proofs
        Establish @O3's cut and closure. Race actor and store commits, capture, provider waves,
        registration and disposal: one store revision, atomic actor reads, no actor revision newer
        than the store, no half cut. Context debt yields retryable ConcurrentCapture and never a
        stale write. Consumers of local, non-persisted, direct and transitive providers fail the
        whole closure with NonDurableContextProvider while the rest of the scope persists; a
        selection with two invalid slots, one transitive, reports both with every detail field.
        Capture invokes no adapter, emits no context event and mutates nothing (a spy store and an
        adapter that fails if called). Planned.
    V4  No-replay restore and the delayed-write crash
        Establish @O4: restored entries passive until bound and stale on activation; value
        outcomes not redelivered for an already delivered value; settled outcomes drained before
        activities; pending commits invoked restore as unknown with the same attempt and queued
        ones as interrupt; streams resubscribe fresh with the latest value kept and terminal lanes
        do not restart; schedules keep deadlines and an overdue one fires exactly once; counters
        never reset and exhausted headroom fails before installation; no lookup, commit, input,
        initial or old emission replays. The decisive scenario, kept from archived Persistence V4
        and adapted to @O6: (1) capture a cut with a commit queued; (2) the pre-invocation write is
        acknowledged and the adapter dispatches remotely; (3) suppress every later write; (4) crash
        and restore exactly those bytes; (5) require unknown with the original attempt.id and no
        new request. Variant: suppress the pre-invocation write itself; require the adapter never
        called and interrupt on both sides of the restart. Planned.
    V5  Layout, ordering, failures and disposal
        Establish @O3 and @O5 with a fault-injecting store: torn record sets and torn manifests at
        every key boundary restore the last good generation; delayed older writes never replace
        newer ones; bursts in one task produce one write and one waiting cut; encode and bound
        failures write nothing; write failures keep committed turns and the last good record and
        report StorageFailure once per reason; issue-only publications schedule no write; dispose
        finalises the last write before releasing the store and waits for it; forget removes
        manifests before records; no key outside the prefix is touched and clear is never called;
        records are byte-identical across captures when unchanged. Run with the memory store, Web
        Storage (jsdom) and the IndexedDB Layer (fake-indexeddb). Planned.
    V6  Attempt durability
        Establish @O6: for a persisted actor the adapter is called only after the write showing the
        commit invoked is acknowledged (an ordering log across the store and the adapter); for a
        non-persisted actor it is called at once; a failed pre-invocation write interrupts the
        commit; retry(attempt) re-issues the same id after restore. Planned.
    V7  Browsers and the IndexedDB Layer
        Establish the real-host parts of @O1 and @O3 in Chromium, Gecko and WebKit (Playwright):
        IndexedDB open failure and quota surface as StorageFailure and the Runtime runs without
        persistence; a reload restores the last good record; a flush on page hide reaches Web
        Storage synchronously; the key prefix digest equals the bundled SHA-256 on Node, Bun, Deno
        and browsers. Planned.
    V8  Entity persistence (entity gate, after S5)
        Establish the entity parts of @O2 and @O4: only confirmed fields that persisted answers
        project are stored; no prediction is ever stored; restore at position 0 with lowered
        frontiers lets live data win; uncertainty markers apply at ready even when the owning actor
        is dormant, and the forced repair runs once. Planned.
    V9  Dormant records and the hydration merge
        Establish @O4's dormant rules with @plan.LazyFeatures.O3 and the merge with
        @plan.ServerRendering.O5: dormant records survive many captures byte-identical; a version
        change discards them; a persisted actor beats a hydrated one and hydrated data beats
        restored data. Planned.
    V10  Proving app
        Establish the whole Plan in references/dispatch-desk on its Next.js App Router host: reload
        restores the session, an editor draft and its pending save as unknown, ticketById entries
        and confirmed Ticket records, in Chromium, Gecko and WebKit. Planned.
questions
    Q1  What happens when a ref's options differ from a restored actor's captured options?
        A ref's retain or context may change in code without a version bump, while the restored
        actor carries the captured options. Recommended: ensure rejects with the Runtime's
        ref-conflict FlowError, remediation "bump persistence.version". Restore cannot know a ref's
        options before ensure, and silently re-wiring a restored actor would break its durable
        closure.
    Q2  Does an application need a public persistence status read?
        Restore discards are evidence only, and write-side failures appear as issues on persisted
        actors. Recommended: no public status in version 1; add one only if a real screen must show
        "your changes are not being saved" outside any persisted actor.
    Q3  Are the scope bounds right?
        4,096 records and 16 MiB per scope, and a write per publishing task without a debounce
        timer, are estimates. Recommended: measure in S6 with the Dispatch Desk queue and an entity
        store of 10,000 records on IndexedDB while typing, and revise the numbers here.
    Q4  Can corrupted or abandoned keys leak?
        KeyValueStore cannot list keys, so record keys named by no decodable manifest are never
        removed. Recommended: accept for version 1 (each leak is bounded by one generation's records,
        and it happens only after corruption or a crash between a manifest write and its cleanup);
        a future listing capability of the IndexedDB Layer could prune them.
